⚠️ **IMPORTANT: `proxyPasswordEnv` without `proxyUsername` parses but the credential is silently never used**

The validation enforces one direction only:

```dart
if (username != null && passwordEnv == null) {
  throw ConfigException(
    '$configLabel: "proxyUsername" without "proxyPasswordEnv" — the '
    'password must be referenced by env name, never inline',
  );
}
```

The mirror case — `proxyPasswordEnv` set, `proxyUsername` omitted — is accepted at parse time, but the password is then dead config:

- http/https: `_wireProxyBasicAuth` returns immediately when `username == null` (`proxy_connector_io.dart:110`), so no credentials are ever registered.
- socks5: `useAuth = username != null`, so the greeting offers only `0x00` (no-auth) and the RFC1929 exchange never happens.

The user's intent is unambiguous (they configured a password), the config passes strict validation, and the failure surfaces later as a proxy-side 407 / RFC1929 rejection — which is fail-closed, but the error text then points the user at `proxyPasswordEnv`, which they believe they already set. Please confirm the intent; if asymmetric config is unintended, reject it in the same block:

```dart
if (passwordEnv != null && username == null) {
  throw ConfigException(
    '$configLabel: "proxyPasswordEnv" without "proxyUsername" — both are '
    'required (HTTP Basic / SOCKS5 RFC1929 send them as a pair)',
  );
}
```

(If anonymous+password proxies are a real thing you want to support, at least reject it for `socks5`, where RFC1929 strictly requires both fields.)

💡 **SUGGESTION: validate the proxy port range at parse time**

`ProviderNetworkConfig.fromYaml` validates scheme, credentials, and host, but not the port: `proxy: http://proxy.corp:99999` parses fine (`Uri.port` accepts >65535) and only fails later at `Socket.connect`/`SecureSocket.connect` with a raw `ArgumentError`/`SocketException` — the fail-closed wrapper will render it, but the message won't name the config key the way the other parse errors do. One line in the factory keeps all config errors equally actionable:

```dart
if (uri.port != 0 && (uri.port < 1 || uri.port > 65535)) {
  throw ConfigException(
    '$configLabel: proxy port ${uri.port} is out of range 1-65535 (got: "$rawProxy")',
  );
}
```

Add the >65535 case to the existing scheme/host rejection UT for symmetry.
